<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-logic-equivalence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Equivalence</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Determine whether statements are logically equivalent.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use equivalent forms of the conditional interchangeably.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Show that statements are equivalent using truth tables.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>
	<introduction>
		<p>
			Two statements are <term>logically equivalent</term><idx>logical equivalence</idx> if they have the same truth value in every possible situation. In other words, no matter the circumstances, both statements are either simultaneously true or simultaneously false. We write this relationship as <m>P \equiv Q</m>. It means that statements <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are interchangeable in logical arguments.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-conditional-equivalence">
		<title>Logical Equivalence and the Conditional</title>
		<p>
			It may seem counterintuitive at first, but the conditional statement <em>if</em> <m>p</m>, <em>then</em> <m>q</m> (<m>p \to q</m>) can be written using negation and an OR statement. To be precise,
		</p>
		<fact>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<md>
						p \to q \equiv \neg p \lor q.
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</fact>
		<p>
			To understand this intuitively, consider the following example. Keep in mind that symbolic logic cannot capture every nuance of English, and we may need to change verb tenses or otherwise rephrase to make equivalent forms clear.
			(For a review of the conditional, see <xref text="global" ref="subsubsec-conditional-truth-table"/>.)
		</p>
		<example>
			<p>
				You are told, <q>If the cookie contains nuts, then Alex cannot eat it.</q> With some thought, you should see that this is equivalent to saying, <q>Either the cookie does not contain nuts, or Alex cannot eat it.</q>
			</p>
		</example>
		<p>
			While verbal examples such as the one above illustrate our intuitive understanding that two statements are equivalent, equivalence is definitively demonstrated with a truth table. One advantage of a truth table is that the result can be shown independently of a problem's specific details.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use a truth table to show that <m>p \to q \equiv \neg p \lor q</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We build a truth table as shown in <xref ref="sec-logic-truth-tables"/>. Begin by adding the standard columns for <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>, then continue by adding a third column for <m>\neg p</m> using column 1. Next, compute <m>\neg p \lor q</m> using columns 3 and 2. We end by adding a column for <m>p \to q</m> using columns 1 and 2.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p \lor q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p\rightarrow q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						Observe that the truth values in columns 4 and 5 match exactly. This demonstrates the equivalence of <m>p \to q</m> with <m>\neg p \lor q</m>.
					</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			The negation of a conditional also has an equivalent form written using AND. That is,
		</p>
		<fact>
			<idx><h>negation</h><h>of a conditional</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<md>
						\neg(p \to q) \equiv p \land \neg q.
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</fact>
		<p>
			You can demonstrate this equivalence using a truth table, much like the previous example; try it in <xref ref="ex-logic-equivalence-negation"/>. The intuitive sense of the above logical statement is illustrated by the following example.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the statement <q>If you park here, then you will get a ticket.</q> What set of conditions would prove this statement false?
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								You don't park here and you get a ticket.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								You don't park here and you don't get a ticket.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								You park here and you don't get a ticket.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The first two situations cannot make the statement false, because it makes no promise about what happens if you park somewhere else. The third statement, however, contradicts the conditional statement <q>If you park here, then you will get a ticket</q> because you parked here but didn't get a ticket.
				</p>
				<p>
					This example demonstrates the general rule shown above that the negation of a conditional can be written as a conjunction: <q>It is not the case that if you park here, then you will get a ticket</q> is equivalent to <q>You park here and you do not get a ticket.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					Notice that the negation is an <em>and</em> statement, not another conditional. A common mistake is to negate the conclusion only, as in <q>If you park here, then you will not get a ticket.</q> That statement is not the negation of the original.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-contrapositive">
		<title>The Contrapositive</title>
		<p>
			<em>And</em> and <em>or</em> statements can have their propositions reversed without affecting the truth value of the statement. If you need to buy beans <em>and</em> eggs, that's the same as buying eggs <em>and</em> beans. The order does matter for a conditional statement, however. Sometimes during an argument, an <em>if, then</em> statement will be reversed or combined with <em>not</em> in different ways. There are names for these combinations, and it is helpful to look at which statements are equivalent to each other. Here are the names for the different combinations of <em>if, then</em> and <em>not</em> statements.
		</p>
		<p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<term>Original Conditional</term>: <m>p\to q</m> (<em>if</em> <m>p</m>, <em>then</em> <m>q</m>)
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<term>Converse</term><idx>converse</idx>: <m>q\to p</m> (<em>if</em> <m>q</m>, <em>then</em> <m>p</m>)
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<term>Inverse</term><idx>inverse</idx>: <m>\neg p\to \neg q</m> (<em>if not</em> <m>p</m>, <em>then not</em> <m>q</m>)
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<term>Contrapositive</term><idx>contrapositive</idx>: <m>\neg q\to \neg p</m> (<em>if not</em> <m>q</m>, <em>then not</em> <m>p</m>)
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</p>
		<p>
			The meaning of each of these forms is examined in the following example.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Assume this original conditional statement is true: <q>If you take your dog to the park, they will be happy.</q> Write the converse, inverse, and contrapositive of the original statement and determine which are equivalent to each other.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							Original Statement: <m>p</m> is <q>You take your dog to the park,</q> and <m>q</m> is <q>Your dog is happy.</q>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Converse: <q>If your dog is happy, then you took them to the park.</q> Notice we needed to change the tense to keep the order of events the same. If your dog is happy, does that mean you took them to the park? Not necessarily, because there are other reasons your dog could be happy like getting a treat or taking a walk. So the converse does not have to be true.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Inverse: <q>If you don't take your dog to the park, they will not be happy.</q> This is similar to the converse. Even if you don't take your dog to the park, they could be happy for another reason. So the inverse does not have to be true. The inverse is logically equivalent to the converse.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Contrapositive: <q>If your dog is not happy, then you did not take them to the park.</q> Since the original statement is true, this is also true. Taking your dog to the park would result in them being happy, so if they are not happy we can say that they did not go to the park. The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			The above example provides intuition for the following fact.
		</p>
		<fact xml:id="fact-logic-equivalence-contrapositive">
			<title>Logical Equivalence of the Contrapositive</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A conditional statement and its contrapositive are equivalent. The converse and inverse of a conditional statement are also equivalent. That is,
					<md>
						<mrow>p \to q \equiv\amp \neg q\to \neg p </mrow>
						<mrow>q \to p \equiv\amp \neg p\to \neg q</mrow>
					</md>
					However, <m>p\to q</m> is <em>not</em> equivalent to its converse <m>q\to p</m> or its inverse <m>\neg p\to \neg q</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</fact>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use a truth table to demonstrate the equivalence of a conditional statement and its contrapositive, and the equivalence of its converse and inverse. That is, verify that
					<md>
						<mrow>p \to q \equiv\amp \neg q\to \neg p</mrow>
						<mrow>q \to p \equiv\amp \neg p\to \neg q.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Here is a truth table with a column for each piece of the statements.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \to q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q\to \neg p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q \to p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p\to \neg q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Note that columns three and six show the same truth values. This demonstrates the equivalence of the conditional with its contrapositive. Similarly, columns seven and eight match, so the converse and the inverse are equivalent. Comparing columns three and seven, the conditional and its converse differ in rows two and three, so they are not equivalent.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-biconditional">
		<title>The Biconditional</title>
		<p>
			In everyday life, we often have a stronger meaning in mind when we use a conditional statement. Consider the statement <q>If you submit your timesheet today, then you will be paid next Friday.</q> What the payroll representative really means is <q>If you submit your timesheet today, then you will be paid next Friday, and if you don't submit your timesheet today, then you won't be paid next Friday.</q>
			Let <m>t</m> be <q>You submit your timesheet today,</q> and let <m>p</m> be <q>You will be paid next Friday.</q> The conditional <em>if</em> <m>t</m>, <em>then</em> <m>p</m> also includes the (unspoken!) inverse: <em>if not</em> <m>t</m>, <em>then not</em> <m>p</m>. Because the inverse is equivalent to the converse (<xref ref="fact-logic-equivalence-contrapositive"/>), this is the same as <em>if</em> <m>p</m>, <em>then</em> <m>t</m>. A more compact way to express this statement is <q>You will be paid next Friday if and only if you submit your timesheet today.</q> A statement of this form is called a <term>biconditional</term>.
		</p>
		<sidebyside widths="60% 34%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
			<p>
				Because a biconditional statement <m>p \leftrightarrow q</m> is equivalent to <m>(p \rightarrow q) \land (q \rightarrow p)</m>, we can think of it as a conditional statement combined with its converse: <em>if</em> <m>p</m>, <em>then</em> <m>q</m>, <em>and</em> <em>if</em> <m>q</m>, <em>then</em> <m>p</m>.
				The double-headed arrow shows that the conditional statement goes from left to right and from right to left. A biconditional is considered true as long as the antecedent and the consequent have the same truth value; that is, they are either both true or both false.<idx><h>truth table</h><h>for a biconditional</h></idx>
			</p>
			<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
				<row header="yes">
					<cell><m>p</m></cell>
					<cell><m>q</m></cell>
					<cell><m>p \leftrightarrow q</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>T</cell>
					<cell>T</cell>
					<cell>T</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>T</cell>
					<cell>F</cell>
					<cell>F</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>F</cell>
					<cell>T</cell>
					<cell>F</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>F</cell>
					<cell>F</cell>
					<cell>T</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</sidebyside>
		<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-logic-biconditional">
			<title>Biconditional</title>
			<idx>biconditional</idx>
			<p>
				A biconditional is a logical conditional statement in which the antecedent and consequent are interchangeable.
			</p>
			<p>
				A biconditional is written as 
				<md>
					p \leftrightarrow q
				</md>
			and is read as <q><m>p</m> if and only if <m>q</m>.</q><idx>if and only if</idx>
			</p>
		</assemblage>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Suppose this statement is true: <q>The garbage truck comes down my street if and only if it is Thursday morning.</q> Which of the following statements could be true?
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							It is noon on Thursday and the garbage truck did not come down my street this morning.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							It is Monday and the garbage truck is coming down my street.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							It is Wednesday at 11:59 p.m. and the garbage truck did not come down my street today.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>p</m> be <q>The garbage truck comes down my street,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>It is Thursday morning.</q>
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							This cannot be true. This is like the third row of the truth table; it is false that the garbage truck came, but it is true that it was Thursday morning.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							This cannot be true. This is like the second row of the truth table; it is true that the garbage truck came, but it is false that it is Thursday morning.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							This could be true. This is like the fourth row of the truth table; it is false that the garbage truck came, and it is also false that it is Thursday morning, so everything worked out like it should.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			The biconditional can also appear in larger truth tables like those in <xref ref="sec-logic-truth-tables"/>, as the next example shows.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Create a truth table for the statement <m>(A\lor B)\leftrightarrow \neg C</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We start by listing all the possible truth value combinations for <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>. After creating those three columns, we create a fourth column for the antecedent, <m>A \lor B</m>, whose values we find by temporarily ignoring the column for <m>C</m> and focusing on <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
					Next, we can create a column for the negation of <m>C</m>. Finally, we find the truth values of <m>(A \lor B) \leftrightarrow \neg C</m>. Remember, a biconditional is true when the truth value of the two parts match, but it is false when the truth values do not match. The resulting table is shown below.
				</p>
				<tabular halign= "center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row  header="yes">
						<cell><m>A</m></cell>
						<cell><m>B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>C</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(A \lor B)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg C</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(A\lor B)\leftrightarrow \neg C</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-deMorgan">
		<title>De Morgan's Laws</title>
		<p>
			In 1847, Augustus De Morgan published two rules of logic that had previously been known only informally. These rules allow us to rewrite the negation of an AND statement with OR, and the negation of an OR statement with AND. 
		</p>
		<p>
			To illustrate the intuitive sense of <term>De Morgan's laws</term>, suppose you want to schedule a meeting with two colleagues at 4:30 p.m. on Friday, and you need both of them to be available at that time. Let <m>A</m> be <q>Colleague A is available,</q> and let <m>B</m> be <q>Colleague B is available.</q> What situation would make it impossible to have the meeting? That is, what would it mean if it were not the case that Colleague A and Colleague B could both meet? Writing this in logic, what is equivalent to <m>\neg(A \land B)</m>?
			This situation is equivalent to either Colleague A not being available or Colleague B not being available. That is, (again writing the result in logic) <m>\neg A \lor \neg B</m> is an equivalent form.
		</p>
		<fact xml:id="fact-logic-deMorgan">
			<title>De Morgan's Laws</title>
			<idx>De Morgan's laws</idx>
			<idx><h>logical rules</h><h>De Morgan's laws</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					For any two propositions <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>,
					<md>
						<mrow>\neg(p \lor q) \equiv\amp \neg p\land \neg q </mrow>
						<mrow>\neg(p \land q) \equiv\amp \neg p\lor \neg q</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</fact>
		<p>
			De Morgan's laws can be demonstrated using truth tables. Try it in <xref ref="ex-logic-equivalence-demorgan-1"/> and <xref ref="ex-logic-equivalence-demorgan-2"/>.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<exercises>
		<!-- Sources: Exercises 1, 2, and 4 are adapted from Benjamin Jackson's
		MATH 105 lecture slides (Objective 6, including clicker questions),
		Walla Walla University, Fall 2026. Exercises 3, 6, and 8 are
		based on his MATH 105 Written Homework 4 (Fall 2026), and
		Exercise 7 on his MATH 105 written-homework question library
		(Fall 2026). Exercises 5, 9, and 10 are new.
		-->
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise><!--  #1 (Obj 6 slides: clouds) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Assume this conditional statement is true: <q>If it is raining, then there are clouds in the sky.</q> Write the converse, inverse, and contrapositive of the statement, and decide which of them must also be true.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							<p>
								Converse: <q>If there are clouds in the sky, then it is raining.</q> This does not have to be true; there are cloudy days without rain.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Inverse: <q>If it is not raining, then there are no clouds in the sky.</q> This does not have to be true, for the same reason.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Contrapositive: <q>If there are no clouds in the sky, then it is not raining.</q> This must be true, because it is equivalent to the original statement.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!--  #2 (Obj 6 clicker: giant cookie) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose this statement is true: <q>If I eat this giant cookie, then I will feel sick.</q> Which of the following statements must also be true?
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								If I feel sick, then I ate the giant cookie.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If I don't eat the giant cookie, then I won't feel sick.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If I don't feel sick, then I didn't eat the giant cookie.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Only (c) must be true; it is the contrapositive of the original statement. Statement (a) is the converse and (b) is the inverse. Neither has to be true, because I could feel sick for some other reason.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!--  #3 (Written Homework 4: 06e) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the statement <q>If you finish your chores, then you can have dessert.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write the converse, the inverse, and the contrapositive of this statement in words.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Write the negation of the original statement in words, without starting your sentence with <q>It is not true that.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Rewrite the original statement as a biconditional (using <q>if and only if</q>), and explain in a sentence how its meaning is different from the original statement.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>p</m> be <q>You finish your chores,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>You can have dessert.</q> The statement is <m>p \to q</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Converse (<m>q \to p</m>): <q>If you can have dessert, then you finished your chores.</q> Inverse (<m>\neg p \to \neg q</m>): <q>If you don't finish your chores, then you can't have dessert.</q> Contrapositive (<m>\neg q \to \neg p</m>): <q>If you can't have dessert, then you didn't finish your chores.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Since <m>\neg(p \to q) \equiv p \land \neg q</m>, the negation is <q>You finish your chores, but you can't have dessert.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<q>You can have dessert if and only if you finish your chores.</q> The biconditional <m>p \leftrightarrow q</m> also says that if you don't finish your chores, then you can't have dessert (the inverse, or equivalently the converse). The original statement says nothing about what happens if you don't finish your chores.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!--  #4 (Obj 6 clicker: greased pan) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which of the following is equivalent to the negation of <q>If you don't grease the pan, then the food will stick to it</q>?
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								You don't grease the pan, and the food doesn't stick to it.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								You don't grease the pan, and the food sticks to it.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								You grease the pan, and the food doesn't stick to it.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The answer is (a). Let <m>g</m> be <q>You grease the pan,</q> and let <m>s</m> be <q>The food sticks to the pan.</q> The statement is <m>\neg g \to s</m>, so its negation is <m>\neg g \land \neg s</m>: you don't grease the pan, and the food doesn't stick. Statement (b) describes the original statement coming true, and (c) negates the wrong part.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="ex-logic-equivalence-negation"><!--  #5 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use a truth table to show that <m>\neg(p \to q) \equiv p \land \neg q</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \to q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg(p \to q)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \land \neg q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Columns four and six match in every row, so the two statements are equivalent.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="ex-logic-equivalence-demorgan-1"><!--  #6 (Written Homework 4: 06a) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					The first of De Morgan's laws is <m>\neg(p \lor q) \equiv \neg p \land \neg q</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Create a truth table that shows this equivalence.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>p</m> be <q>You drink coffee,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>You eat breakfast.</q> Use De Morgan's law to write two sentences with equivalent meaning. (Think about this carefully, as a literal translation might be awkward and unclear.)
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								See the truth table below. The last two columns match in every row, so the two statements are equivalent.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Answers will vary, for example <q>It is not true that you drink coffee or eat breakfast</q> (more smoothly, <q>You neither drink coffee nor eat breakfast.</q>) and <q>You don't drink coffee, and you don't eat breakfast.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \lor q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg(p \lor q)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p \land \neg q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="ex-logic-equivalence-demorgan-2"><!--  #7 (question library: 06b) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					The second of De Morgan's laws is <m>\neg(p \land q) \equiv \neg p \lor \neg q</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>p</m> be <q>Jordan is at the game,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>Priya is at the game.</q> Use De Morgan's law to write two sentences with equivalent meaning.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use a truth table to show that the second of De Morgan's laws must be true.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Answers will vary, for example <q>Jordan and Priya are not both at the game</q> and <q>Either Jordan is not at the game, or Priya is not at the game.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								See the truth table below. The last two columns match in every row, so the two statements are equivalent.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \land q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg(p \land q)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p \lor \neg q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!--  #8 (Written Homework 4: 06d) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A friend claims that the exclusive or <m>p \oplus q</m> (see <xref ref="ex-logic-xor"/>) is equivalent to <m>(p \land \neg q) \lor (\neg p \land q)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>p</m> be <q>You can have fries with your meal,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>You can have a baked potato with your meal.</q> Argue whether or not your friend is correct.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use a truth table to check your conclusion to (a).
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Your friend is correct. If a server says <q>You can have fries or a baked potato,</q> they mean the <em>or</em> exclusively: you can have one or the other, but not both. This is exactly what the right-hand side says: <q>You can have fries and not a baked potato, or a baked potato and not fries.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								See the truth table below. The last two columns match in every row. They also match <m>(p \lor q) \land \neg(p \land q)</m> from <xref ref="ex-logic-xor"/>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \land \neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg p \land q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(p \land \neg q) \lor (\neg p \land q)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \oplus q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!--  #9 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose this statement is true: <q>The store is open if and only if it is a weekday.</q> Which of the following statements could be true?
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								It is Saturday, and the store is open.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								It is Tuesday, and the store is closed.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								It is Sunday, and the store is closed.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>p</m> be <q>The store is open,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>It is a weekday.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								This cannot be true. It is like the second row of the truth table for <m>p \leftrightarrow q</m>: the store is open, but it is not a weekday.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								This cannot be true. It is like the third row: the store is not open, but it is a weekday.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								This could be true. It is like the fourth row: the store is not open, and it is not a weekday.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!--  #10 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Create a truth table for <m>p \leftrightarrow \neg q</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Compare your result with the truth table for the exclusive or in <xref ref="ex-logic-xor"/>. What do you notice?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \leftrightarrow \neg q</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					The last column is the same as the column for the exclusive or, <m>p \oplus q</m>. So <q><m>p</m> or <m>q</m>, but not both</q> means the same as <q><m>p</m> if and only if not <m>q</m>.</q>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>